  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A storm system will bring a prolonged period of rain \nto much of southern New England Thursday through Saturday with \nperiods of snow in the higher elevations. Significant accumulation \nis not expected.\n\nLight radar returns are visible across central SNE early this \nmorning as the first taste of the rain to come later today \noverspreads the region. This, as a strengthening low pressure center \nand lagging mid level trough will track over the Mid-Atlantic today \nand move off the coast (south of New England) tonight. Eventually it \nlifts into the Gulf of Maine Friday night into the weekend taking \nits associated moisture with it. Thus, the next 36-42 hours are \nexpected to be wet before we dry out for the weekend. Low/mid level \nwarm advection ahead of the low will bring rather widespread rain \ntoday, mainly confined to the interior/NW SNE under favorable \nE/upslope flow off the water with a moderate LLJ. Rain then \noverspreads the rest of the region this evening as the low moves \nsouth of Long Island. As this low moves further east flow becomes N \nthen NW pulling in cooler air into the previously relatively mild \nboundary layer. This cold advection combined with potential diabatic \ncooling in any heavier bands of precipitation will be able to flip \nprecipitation over to a wet snow for the higher elevations in the \nBerkshires and to a lesser extent the Worcester Hills. Outside of \nthese areas, a cold rain is expected. Fortunately precipitation \nshould be exiting/sinking south by late Friday so as the colder \novernight temps arrive precipitation should be coming to an end.\nFor this reason, while snow is expected to be confided to the high \nelevations, can't rule out a period of light snow further south into \nCT or the RI hills late in the day Friday though it is a low \nprobability scenario. Overall, this is expected to be a non-event \nfor most of the region, and a very low impact event in the high \nelevations with accumulations of a coating (Worcester Hills) to a \nfew inches (Berkshires) most likely on grassy surfaces. Given the \ntrajectory of the wind on Friday, temperatures will struggle to get \nout of the upper 30s in the high elevations and the mid to upper 40s \ntoward the south coast. \n\nOne more thing to note, with regard to coastal flooding potential. \nWhile tides are not astronomically high, guidance does indicate \npotential for ~1-1.5 ft of surge. While not an issue for most of the \ncoast, this would put Nantucket Harbor within reach of minor flood \nlevels during Friday afternoon's high tide. If this signal persists \ncoastal flood headlines may be needed. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure brings cool and dry conditions back to \nsouthern New England for a majority of the weekend and into next \nweek.\n\nLatest round of guidance has trended the low pushing offshore into \nAtlantic Canada earlier than previously anticipated... likely \nsometime Saturday instead of Sunday. High pressure then builds over \nthe Great Lakes this weekend. The tighter pressure gradient will \nallow blustery conditions to linger through Sunday, but the \nstrengthening high will mark the return of a dry period through the \nstart of the next work week. High temps slowly climb through the \nearly week... in the upper 40s Saturday to the mid 50s \nTuesday/Wednesday. The high pressure will also bring back nights of \nradiational cooling, potentially dropping low temperatures into the \n20s through mid week as well.",
